\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_regime_transition.pdf}
\caption{Predictor relevance across panel sizes. $\eta_{\max}$ significance (orange) drops from 5/12 to 0--1/12, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (blue) remains consistently high (8--8--7/12). Shaded region: $K^* \in (3, 5)$. Bootstrap: $B{=}9999$, Rademacher, max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$.}
\label{fig:regime_transition}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Weakest-Link Dominance at \texorpdfstring{$K{=}3$}{K=3}}
\label{sec:dominance}

Table~\ref{tab:dominance} reports the log-log OLS regression (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}) for all 12 conditions across the 455 panels ($K{=}3$, 15 judges from Table~\ref{tab:models}).

Under OLS, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is FDR-significant in all 12/12 conditions (positive in 10, negative in 2 position-bias conditions). $\eta_{\max}$ is significant in 10/12 (exceptions: MMLU $\times$ score manipulation and ARC $\times$ position bias), consistent with the weakest-link monotonicity property (\S\ref{sec:threat_model}). Dominance analysis yields 10 co-dominant, 2 $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-dominant, and zero $\eta_{\max}$-dominant conditions. However, the $r = 0.70$ $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$--$\eta_{\max}$ correlation raises confound concerns, and OLS ignores the non-independence of the 455 panels (Appendix~\ref{app:keff_dist}).

Figure~\ref{fig:robustness} compares OLS against three corrections: both predictors attenuate under bootstrap ($\eta_{\max}$ 5/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 8/12), with clustered SE most conservative ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12). Crossed random-effects analysis confirms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance at $K{\geq}5$ (Appendix~\ref{app:crossed_re}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
OLS                    & 10/12 & 12/12 \\
Clustered SE           & 4/12  & 0/12  \\
Mixed effects          & 7/12  & 3/12  \\
Wild bootstrap         & 5/12  & 8/12  \\
Frac.\ logit (clust.)  & 8/12  & 10/12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Inference method sensitivity at $K{=}3$ (455 panels, 12 conditions). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS); positive direction is preserved in 10/12 conditions across all methods.}
\label{tab:method_comparison}
\end{table}

Power analysis yields MDE $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power (Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}); positive coefficient direction (10/12) is preserved across all methods.

\subsection{The Diminishing Weakest Link Across Panel Sizes}
\label{sec:cross_k}

The $K{=}3$ pattern is method-sensitive (Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison}), contrasting with $K{\geq}5$ where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association is consistent (bootstrap 8/12; fractional logit 11/12). We repeat the bootstrap at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ with max-$p$ correction.

Meta-analytic pooling \citep{dersimonian1986meta} quantifies trajectories independent of significance thresholds. $\eta_{\max}$'s pooled $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ declines monotonically: $0.243$ [$0.163, 0.323$] at $K{=}3$, $0.091$ [$-0.031, 0.212$] at $K{=}5$, $0.086$ [$-0.039, 0.211$] at $K{=}7$; CI crosses zero at $K{\geq}5$. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s pooled coefficient remains positive ($0.286$--$0.353$, CI excluding zero; Appendix~\ref{app:meta_analytic_details}). Figure~\ref{fig:regime_transition} shows significance counts; per-condition effect sizes with 95\% CIs are in Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot} (Appendix~\ref{app:effect_size_forest}). The $\eta_{\max}$ decline holds under both $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity.


Random faults yield $R^2{=}0.730$; adaptive attack reduces predictability ($R^2{=}0.285$ at $K{=}5$) but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains significance (7/12), suggesting diverse judges present distinct attack surfaces rather than redundant defenses (Appendix~\ref{app:rf_contrast_detail}).

\subsection{Attack-Type Heterogeneity}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM bootstrap confirms positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ association in 10/12 conditions with no magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}). The two exceptions are position bias: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient is negative in both benchmarks ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = -0.334, -0.372$), indicating that diverse panels are \emph{less} susceptible. Because position bias is a shared vulnerability where all judges exhibit the same directional preference, independent judges provide genuine redundancy---consistent with the Condorcet intuition and the targeted-vs-shared distinction in the attack taxonomy.

\subsection{Aggregation Rules and the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{$\eta_{\max}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sig} & \textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ $\beta$ dir.} & \textbf{$R^2$} \\
\midrule
Threshold       & 12/12 & 9/12  & 8+/1$-$   & 0.745 \\
Majority        & 10/12 & 12/12 & 10+/2$-$  & 0.349 \\
Weighted        & 9/12  & 12/12 & 10+/2$-$  & 0.336 \\
Bayesian        & 8/12  & 6/12  & 5+/1$-$   & 0.136 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
